\subsection{Experimental Design}
\label{sec:exp_design}

We conduct two complementary experimental paradigms.

\paragraph{Experiment 1: Count Curves.}
We vary the number of types presented in the prompt schema to measure performance as a function of schema size. For each count level, we sample 25 random type batches for Few-NERD and FewRel (5 for DocRED and MAVEN)\footnote{DocRED and MAVEN use fewer batches due to higher per-batch evaluation cost (document-level RE and 8{,}042 sentences, respectively).} and evaluate the same 1{,}000 test samples across all batches. At count${}=1$, every type is evaluated individually as a solo baseline. The remaining count levels follow natural partition points in each schema's type hierarchy rather than uniform spacing: \{2, 6, 12, 22, 44, 66\} for Few-NERD, \{4, 8, 16, 32, 40, 64, 80\} for FewRel, \{4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 96\} for DocRED, and \{6, 12, 22, 44, 64\} for MAVEN.

\paragraph{Experiment 2: BCS-Controlled.}
We fix type count and vary BCS to isolate confusability from schema size. For each count level, we construct one low-BCS batch, one high-BCS batch, and 25 random batches (27 total). Extreme batches are constructed via greedy search over the pairwise SCS matrix: the low-BCS batch starts from the least-similar type pair and iteratively adds the type with lowest maximum similarity to already-selected types; the high-BCS batch mirrors this with maximum similarity. We evaluate on Few-NERD (count$\,\in\{6, 12\}$), FewRel (count$\,\in\{8, 16\}$, both 16-relation (pilot) and 80-relation pools), and DocRED (count$\,\in\{16, 32\}$). Within-pool 16-relation subsets from the 80-relation pool isolate confusability from pool composition. MAVEN results are reported separately due to a centrality confound (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}).

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

\textbf{NER}: text-match F1, where a prediction is correct if the exact text string matches after lowercasing and whitespace normalization, \emph{and} the type matches; partial spans count as incorrect. \textbf{RE}: accuracy (exact match of predicted relation label to gold). Each batch evaluates only samples whose gold relation is included in the candidate set; out-of-schema samples are excluded before inference. The prompt allows a \texttt{none} response for cases where the model judges no candidate applies; any such prediction is scored as incorrect since the gold is always among the candidates by construction. \textbf{ED}: relaxed F1, where any token overlap between predicted and gold trigger spans, with correct event type.


\subsection{Model and Inference}
\label{sec:model}

\looseness=-1
We use Qwen3-32B-FP8 \citep{qwen3-2025} served via vLLM v0.20.2 \citep{vllm-2023} on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPU (96\,GB) with greedy decoding (temperature${}\,=\,0$, seed 42). Two NER/ED prompt versions are evaluated: v1 requests character offsets (evaluated via text matching); v2 adds format constraints that double precision with minimal recall loss. RE uses a single prompt version throughout. Both versions produce consistent degradation patterns; we report both where applicable to quantify formatting sensitivity \citep{sclar-sensitivity-2024} (templates in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompts}). Cross-model validation uses Meta-Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct (FP8) and Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct \citep{llama3-2024}. All experiments use single-seed evaluation with greedy decoding, which substantially reduces model-level randomness (residual SD = 0.88\,pp at count 80 under greedy decoding, likely attributable to FP8 quantization non-determinism); bootstrap CIs capture the remaining sampling variance from batch composition. Pair-level CIs use percentile bootstrap ($B{=}10{,}000$); batch-level CIs use $B{=}1{,}000$.

